abstract: >-
  Behavior Tracker is a desktop research software platform for video-based
  analysis of rodent experiments. It combines a React and TypeScript interface,
  a Tauri and Rust backend, SQLite persistence, and Python processing pipelines
  for Open Field Test, Forced Swim Test, and Radial Arm Maze workflows. Users
  configure experimental geometry, calibrate spatial measurements, process
  videos, inspect tracking quality, annotate observations, and export results
  with provenance records. Local CPU processing uses OpenCV segmentation and
  temporal association to estimate body silhouettes, bounding boxes,
  trajectories, and motion descriptors. Optional neural workflows use a Wistar
  Faster R-CNN detector and DeepLabCut HRNet-W32 pose estimation with 27
  keypoints, locally or through Google Colab; the separate Forced Swim neural
  pipeline uses SuperAnimal Quadruped pose estimation. Open Field analysis
  includes distance, speed, center and periphery occupancy, entries, operational
  immobility, and spatial coverage, subject to calibration, observation quality,
  and temporal continuity. Forced Swim analysis provides visual presence and
  activity descriptors per cylinder, with behavioral categories left
  unclassified until human annotation. Radial Arm Maze workflows support
  apparatus configuration, experimental tracking, and review; arm-choice and
  memory-error calculations require explicit arm and gate geometry and
  continuous confirmed observations. Human annotations and corrections are
  retained separately from automated observations. Depending on the workflow,
  exports include frame-level detections or poses, quality indicators, review
  histories, metrics, audit videos, and manifests identifying inputs, parameters,
  and model assets. The repository also implements experimental rearing
  annotation, feature extraction, and model-training tools; the distributed
  rearing classifier manifest is untrained. These capabilities support research
  workflows and do not establish independent scientific validation or
  generalizable behavioral classification accuracy.
